FINAL ANSWER:
**Top owner of surviving lines, by file type** (HEAD @ 62628c0, 18 files; 1,662 non-blank lines, 1,995 counting blank lines)

| Type | Files | Lines (non-blank / with blank) | Top owner | Their lines (share) | With blank (share) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | 7 | 1,032 / 1,202 | Troy Goode (2 addresses, merged) | 564 (54.7%) | 676 (56.2%) |
| `.yml` | 6 | 314 / 375 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 101 (32.2%) | 127 (33.9%) |
| `.md` | 2 | 253 / 341 | Troy Goode | 71 (28.1%) | 116 (34.0%) |
| `.json` | 1 | 40 / 40 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 (50.0%) | 20 (50.0%) |
| no extension (LICENSE etc.) | 2 | 23 / 27 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 21 (91.3%) | 21 (77.8%) |

**Runners-up**
- **`.js`:** Douglas Christopher Wilson has 362 lines (35.1%), or 394 (32.8%) counting blank lines.
- **`.yml`:** Íñigo Marquínez Prado has 80 lines (25.5%), or 95 (25.3%) counting blank lines. Sebastian Beltran has 53 (16.9%).
- **`.md`:** Jon Church has 59 lines (23.3%), or 72 (21.1%) counting blank lines.
- **`.json`:** Troy Goode has 12 lines (30.0%).

**Merged vs unmerged Troy Goode**
- Troy Goode appears under two addresses (`<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example`). The tool rates the match as strong, but it is a guess from names and addresses. I merged them in memory only; nothing was written to `.mailmap`.
- **`.js` (headline):** counting both addresses as one person, Troy Goode has 564 lines (54.7%). Unmerged, `<email redacted>` has 520 (50.4%) and `troy@laptop.example` has 44 (4.3%).
- **`.md` and `.json`:** no merged figure differs, because only the gmail address appears there.
- **Wilson in the no-extension group:** the 91.3% figure rests on 23 lines in two files, so it is a small sample.

**Bus factor per type** (smallest number of authors holding 50% of that type's lines)
- **`.js`:** 1 (Troy Goode, merged). Unmerged it is also 1, with `<email redacted>` at 50.4%. The active bus factor is 1, counted on the 564 lines of the one active author. 45.3% of the lines belong to people with no commit in the last 365 days. Verdict: single point.
- **`.yml`:** 2 (Wilson and Marquínez Prado, 57.6%). The active bus factor is 2 (Beltran and Gascón), counted on the 132 lines of the 3 active authors. 58.0% of the lines belong to inactive people. Verdict: orphaned.
- **`.md`:** 2 (Goode and Church, 51.4%). The active bus factor is 2, with 43.1% of lines belonging to inactive people.
- **`.json`:** 1 (Wilson, 50.0%). The active bus factor is 1 (Goode), with 55.0% of lines belonging to inactive people. Verdict: single point.
- **No extension:** 1 (Wilson, 91.3%). Verdict: single point and orphaned, as two separate findings.

**What the figures leave out or adjust**
- **Reformat commit set aside:** commit 62628c0 ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes") rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 files, formatting only. Its lines went back to the earlier authors.
  - Wilson overall is 551 lines (33.2%) with the correction and 376 (22.6%) without.
  - Goode overall is 605 (36.4%) with it and 510 (30.7%) without. Merged, that is 649 (39.0%) against 554 (33.3%).
- **Bots:** `dependabot[bot]` is excluded (10 lines, 11 commits), and its lines are not credited to anyone.
- **Same person, one address:** Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (`<email redacted>`) also appears as `alex94puchades`. The tool counts them as one, which gives 30 `.js` lines.
- **Credited to the author, not the committer:** several CI and docs commits were committed by GitHub, so their lines go to the author. Examples are Marquínez Prado (60 lines of de19afc), Beltran (53 lines of 14cefd4) and Mangas (35 lines of f442e77).
- **More mechanical rewrites I did not set aside:** the tool lists 7 commits that look mechanical. The largest is 739dc11 "docs: reformat license" by Wilson, with 17 surviving lines. Two of the seven are not named in the output. I did not inspect them or re-run without them.
- **Copy detection:** the figures use `git blame -w -M -C`.
- **Out of scope:** these counts measure surviving lines, not quality, review work or who understands the code.
